WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.920
Speaker 1: Did Donald Trump as president have&nbsp;
the right to unilaterally declassify documents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.400
just based on his say so? That's one of the&nbsp;
conversations we've been having ever since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.600
FBI raid on Donald Trump's home in Florida last&nbsp;
week took place. And we know that the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.000
right wing, they've been throwing everything at&nbsp;
the wall to see what sticks as it pertains to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.440
the FBI raid in Mar a Lago, saying everything&nbsp;
from this is a witch hunt to the FBI, planted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.720
documents on Donald Trump. But one of the more&nbsp;
pernicious claims made in right wing media circles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.840
lately has been that even if Donald Trump did&nbsp;
have classified documents in his possession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.560
in his basement in Mar a Lago, that's A-OK,&nbsp;
because presidents at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:46.080
are the ultimate authority as to what is&nbsp;
classified information and what gets declassified.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.120
So it doesn't matter. It's kind of a get out of&nbsp;
jail free card if the president does it. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.720
not illegal, as Richard Nixon once said. Here's&nbsp;
one such example of this claim being made by Kash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.440
Patel, who is a former Trump administration&nbsp;
official who now works over at Truth Sociale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.280
also known as Truth Central. He made the claim&nbsp;
to Fox News's Maria BARTIROMO over the weekend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.920
on her Sunday show. Let's take a look at that.
Speaker 6: In October of 2020, he issued a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.640
sweeping declassification order for every&nbsp;
Russiagate document and every single Hillary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.560
Clinton document. Then, on the way out of the&nbsp;
White House, he issued further declassification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:26.000
orders, declassifying whole sets of documents.&nbsp;
And this is a key fact that most Americans are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.520
missing. President Trump as a sitting president&nbsp;
is a unilateral authority for declassification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.000
He can literally stand over a set of documents&nbsp;
and say these are now declassified and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.400
is done with definitive action immediately.
Speaker 1: So Kash Patel here in this video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.800
makes it sound like there's almost a magic spell&nbsp;
involved. Like Donald Trump could just wave his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.240
hand over the documents in question and then,&nbsp;
poof, they are no longer classified information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.880
So nothing to be seen here. Nothing to be&nbsp;
investigated. Well, you may be shocked to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:00.400
that this is not actually how the process works.&nbsp;
Presidents do have the ability to declassify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.320
certain types of information, but there's a&nbsp;
procedure attached to it. Certain executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.280
branch officials have the authority to deem&nbsp;
information as confidential, secret or top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.960
secret. And if it's ultra top secret, then the&nbsp;
document will get the CIA designation. Some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.280
documents in Donald Trump's possession had this&nbsp;
very distinction. There are also rules limiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.400
how the documents can be stored, physically&nbsp;
transported or electronically transmitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:29.760
when someone with the proper authority moves to&nbsp;
declassify the documents. The executive branch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:35.360
has a process to involve the other agencies that&nbsp;
might be affected. And certainly the president is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.640
one of the people who can move to make something&nbsp;
declassified. It's rarely done, but it can happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.800
However, they need to undergo this very&nbsp;
same process and it's not as easy as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.960
waving a magic wand as capital would have&nbsp;
you believe. Here's former CIA director and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.800
former Defense Secretary Leon Panetta, who would&nbsp;
know a thing or two about this very topic being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.440
even more frank than I have been about the&nbsp;
process for how to declassify documents.&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.960
Speaker 7: So you're obviously very well versed&nbsp;
in dealing with classified material. Former Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.640
aides are now trying to claim that there was a&nbsp;
standing order by the president, Donald Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.160
to declassify any document that left&nbsp;
the West Wing for Trump's president.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.840
Is that how declassification works, even with&nbsp;
some of the nation's most guarded secrets?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:26.640
You know, that's nonsense. And and he knows&nbsp;
it. The reality is that there is a process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:34.800
for declassifying information. And if presidents&nbsp;
want to declassify, they have to follow that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:41.280
process, which basically requires that it be&nbsp;
referred to the agencies that are responsible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:47.280
classifying that material. They have something&nbsp;
to say as to whether or not that material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:56.960
should be declassified. So there is nothing that&nbsp;
I'm aware of that indicates that a formal step was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:02.800
taken by this president to, in fact, declassify&nbsp;
anything. Right now, this is pretty much.&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.480
Speaker 1: B.S. It's all a bunch&nbsp;
of B.S., says Leon Panetta.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.560
And then there's an added layer of security&nbsp;
when you're talking about nuclear documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.360
which Donald Trump is accused of having in his&nbsp;
possession as documents about nuclear material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:20.560
are subject to the Atomic Energy Act, which the&nbsp;
president does not have unilateral authority over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.760
Now, secondary to this entire discussion is that&nbsp;
even if the documents were unclassified or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.600
if they were deemed declassified by Donald Trump&nbsp;
right before he left office with his magic wand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.400
it wouldn't serve to get the former president&nbsp;
out of hot water because none of the things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:42.560
Donald Trump is being investigated for require&nbsp;
the documents be classified. The Espionage Act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.040
violations, concealing documents and obstruction&nbsp;
of justice are the three things that the FBI is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.920
looking into as it pertains to Donald Trump. Those&nbsp;
are not dependent on whether the information that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:57.680
Trump had in his possession were classified. He's&nbsp;
not supposed to be taking, hiding or destroying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.160
any of the presidential records regardless of&nbsp;
their classification status. And the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.120
asked him to return the items he didn't willingly&nbsp;
hand them over. He didn't give back all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.400
documents anyway. And so it doesn't matter whether&nbsp;
or not they were classified. Final point on this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.120
because the right has been talking nonstop&nbsp;
about Hillary Clinton and her emails over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.840
the past couple of weeks. They used to say that&nbsp;
Hillary Clinton, she could not be trusted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.400
handle secret information, therefore she could&nbsp;
not be president because when she was secretary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:32.720
of state, she had secret documents in her Gmail&nbsp;
accounts that was being operated through a private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.240
server and it was grossly negligent. We just can't&nbsp;
trust her with these top secret documents. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.120
if that was ever your concern, but you also now&nbsp;
have this position that the president can just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.840
declassify whatever they want on a whim. Doesn't&nbsp;
this render the point that they were making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:52.720
about Hillary in 2016 completely moot? Like, why&nbsp;
would it matter that Hillary Clinton supposedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:57.840
wasn't able to be trusted with classified&nbsp;
information if she would have the power as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.800
president to just declassify whatever she wants&nbsp;
anyway? But the contradictions we know when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.640
comes to the right wing, they don't matter. It's&nbsp;
all about twisting and turning yourself. Anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.040
that you can do to defend Donald Trump no&nbsp;
matter how ridiculous it makes you look.

